We’ve written an FAQ to answer some of the questions we've received about watermarking. 

In summary:

• We’re implementing watermarking to comply with the EU AI Act. Other major model developers have signed the same Code of Practice and will also be implementing watermarking;
• Our watermarking method doesn’t have any practical impact on the quality or content of Claude’s outputs;
• The difference between watermarked and un-watermarked text will not be distinguishable to readers;
• Nothing is added to the text and there are no hidden characters;
• Watermarking doesn’t require extra tokens, and will not be more expensive;
• Watermarks can’t be traced to a specific person, organization, or chat.

Read more: https://t.co/G76iUOJ7Hu

@NickADobos @AnthropicAI @alexalbert__ I can only really engage on the technical part. There is a lot of technical literature on SynthId, this is mostly just recapping it. It's not truly novel technology. The example we chose was contrived, but the math is real. Is there a particular part of it that doesn't make sense that I can walk through more with you?

Your other concerns are valid concerns about AI as a whole as a method of control, but watermarking is  a poor tool for that type of control. You can't guide it towards a certain message. 

My personal belief is that we should advocate for more transparency into both private AI companies and our institutions that govern them.

@NickADobos &gt; humans should be able to steer models 

&gt; we put an invisible hand that steers it to conform to a statistical pattern but don’t worry you still are steering 

😩

@MTSlive @mehul I think about this a lot. My Tesla saves my life, and I trust my life to my Tesla robot. So when Tesla comes out with another robot, I'll probably buy it. That is not true of other companies that I don't trust my life to. 

It is a hard path to make a car company, gain people's trust with your robots, and then expand out. Matic doesn't have that advantage; they have to build that same trust from scratch without having a car, which is much tougher. 

But we love ours, so the same rules apply: we would probably buy another from Matic.

@PaulJeffries Well, it's something we're all going to have to deal with as it changes. Yeah, it'll be interesting to watch the quality of these things as time goes on. 

Five years from now, I can't imagine I'm going to be able to tell what's human-made and what's AI-made anymore. Right now, you can still sort of tell because the AI ones are so lame. 

How long will that stay a problem? Not long, I have a feeling.

@ekuyda We just got ours. So far, she hasn't hated it enough to rip it out of the bed. We'll see how the next couple of weeks go, but so far, we're okay. 

I tried it really hot and then really cold, and discovered neither really worked for me, so I'm floating in between. I just turned on the AI choice for temperature, so it'll be interesting to see how that changes our experience. 

So far, I love it. My wife is not real evangelistic about it yet, but it's better than a lot of things I've brought into the house for her to try.

